OpenABL (https://github.com/OpenHandheldCollective/OpenABL), v%VERSION% from
the PB-OS fork (https://github.com/project-barry/OpenABL): upstream v1.1 plus a
fix for an intermittent hang on the logo before the kernel starts.
GPL-3.0-or-later, Copyright (C) 2026-present Open Handheld Collective.
LICENSE, LICENSES/ and LICENSES.md in this folder are OpenABL's.
Source: openabl-v%VERSION%-source.tar.xz on
https://github.com/project-barry/OpenABL/releases/tag/v%VERSION%
The scripts follow ROCKNIX's OpenABL scripts (GPL-2.0, ROCKNIX contributors);
the per-SoC folders follow Armada (https://github.com/armada-os/armada).

Already using ROCKNIX ABL 1.2? It keeps working with PB-OS; switching is
optional. The PB-OS Installer app (Tools -> Repair) can switch it to OpenABL.

Flashing the wrong ABL can brick the device. Match the SoC carefully:

  SM8550 - Retroid Pocket 6, Retroid Pocket Nova, AYN Thor
  SM8650 - KONKR Pocket FIT (8 Gen 3)

1. Copy the openabl folder from this card to the root of Android
   internal storage.

2. In Android settings, use "Run script as root": browse to your SoC's
   folder (e.g. openabl/SM8550) and run backup_abl.sh. It saves both
   slots as abl_a.img and abl_b.img in that folder and checks them.

3. Copy abl_a.img and abl_b.img to your PC and keep them.
   restore_backup_abl.sh puts them back if you ever want the old ABL.
   Going back to ROCKNIX ABL: set the device model and boot mode again
   in its menu (OpenABL moves those settings out of ROCKNIX's storage).

4. Run flash_abl.sh the same way. It needs the backups from step 2,
   writes both slots and reads each one back. If it reports an error,
   don't reboot: run restore_backup_abl.sh.

5. Power on holding Volume Down to open the OpenABL menu
   (Volume Up/Down to move, Power to select):
     - Device model: your exact model (e.g. "Retroid Pocket Nova")
     - Boot mode: Linux
     - Start
   Coming from ROCKNIX ABL, OpenABL takes over your device model and
   boot mode once.

Android: holding Volume Up at power-on boots Android while the boot mode
is Linux, or set Boot mode to Android in the menu.

Boot source Auto starts PB-OS from internal storage first. With PB-OS
on internal storage, set Boot source to SD card to start from the card.

WARNING: "Uninstall CFW" in the menu erases PB-OS from internal storage
(the ROCKNIX, STORAGE and HOME partitions) AND Android's user data (apps,
photos, accounts). Don't pick it unless you mean to remove PB-OS.
